  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nThe low-level stratus deck that resided over the western half of the \narea this morning has continued to erode away this morning into the \nearly afternoon. Winds today will be light and southerly, thanks to a \nsurface high pressure center situated in the northern Plains. High \ntemperatures today will be on the cooler side, with highs in the upper \n40s, potentially peaking into the low 50s. Tonight, lows are expected \ndrop off into the mid to upper 20s.\n\nThese chilly temperatures will continue through the weekend, with highs \nin the 40s to low 50s, as another trough makes its way from the west \ncoast. This trough is expected to cause pressure falls over the \nsouthern Great plains, bringing low to midlevel clouds and \npreciptiation chances to the region Satudray night into Sunday \nmorning. Recent model guidance has continued to keep the bulk of\nthis precipitation to the south of the area, though a few \nshowers could extend as far north as Interstate 80 (roughly \n15-40% chance south of I- 80). \n\nAs this trough moves off to the east, an upper-level ridge will build \nin across the central CONUS. This pattern aloft will support a dry, \nwarming trend through the beginning of the work week. High temperatures \nare expected to increase into the 50s and even low 60s by Wednesday, \nwhich is quite unusual for mid November (climatological highs are \nnormally closer to 50). As a surface high pressure center in the \nMidwest dominates the flow in the region, breezy southerly winds will \nreturn to the area. These winds are expected to increase in magnitude \nthrough about Wednesday, with gusts of 30-35 MPH possible Monday \nincreasing to 40-45 MPH Wednesday afternoon as the next system \napproaches the area. \n\nA more active weather pattern looks to return mid to late next week, as \na series of troughs cross the Rocky Mountains. The first looks to come \nWednesday evening into Thursday, as a low pressure center developing in \nthe northern plains. This developing cyclone will help support the \nincreasing wind speeds Tuesday and Wednesday, and bring the chance of \nprecipitation back into the area Wednesday night into Thursday. Along \nwith these precipitation chances, a cold front is forecast to cross \nthrough the area Wednesday night, though there is some \nuncertainty in how temperatures will respond in the wake of \nthis front, as current model guidance is suggesting that \ntemperatures rise back up into the 50s Thursday afternoon. \nAdditionally, another trough looks to move into the region at \nthe end of next week, bringing with it a greater precipitation \npotential. However, there is quite a bit of uncertainty \nregarding the timing and location of this system, as the GEFS \n(and parent GFS model) brings this system in faster than the ENS\n(and parent ECMWF model).",
